src/test/prop/DESIGN

Design of the property-based test harness
=========================================

This document explains how the property-based test (PBT) harness is put
together and why.  README covers how to run it and when to use it; this is the
under-the-covers companion.


Goals and constraints
======================

1. Optional and opt-in.  PostgreSQL must build and test exactly as before when
   the feature is off.  The engine is an external dependency with prebuilt
   binaries for a limited set of platforms, so it cannot be a mandatory part of
   the tree.

2. C, not C++.  PostgreSQL is a C project maintained by C programmers.  The
   engine, libhegel, exposes a stable C ABI; everything in this directory is
   plain C that calls it.  No C++ appears in the tree.

3. Fits the existing test world.  When enabled, the suite is just more TAP
   tests, discovered and run by "meson test" / "make check-world" like any
   other.

4. Reproducible.  Every run has a seed (printed if not supplied); every failure
   prints a replay blob and shrinks to a minimal counterexample.


What Hegel provides
===================

libhegel is the engine behind Hegel, shipped as a shared library (libhegel_c)
with a C ABI declared in hegel.h.  It owns the hard parts of property-based
testing: input generation, the shrinking search, and the example database.  The
harness drives it through a small slice of that ABI:

* hegel_settings_* -- configure a run (test-case count, seed, verbosity,
  database path and key).

* hegel_run_start / hegel_next_test_case / hegel_run_result -- the run loop: the
  engine hands us one test case at a time; we execute the property body against
  it and report the outcome with hegel_mark_complete.

* hegel_generate_integer / _float / _boolean / _bytes, and
  hegel_string_generator_text + hegel_generate_string -- the generators the
  property body draws from.

* hegel_run_result_failure / hegel_failure_reproduction_blob -- the minimal
  counterexample and its replay blob after a failing run.

* hegel_test_case_from_blob -- replay a counterexample for reporting.

The engine runs in-process: there is no separate server process and it starts no
threads of its own.  One property run is single-threaded.


Shape of the tree
=================

    pg_prop.h           public harness API: the PG_PROP() macro, draws,
                        assume/note/fail, isolate(), and the Tier-B SQL helpers.
    pg_prop.c           the libhegel driver, generators, CLI, TAP output; main().
    pg_prop_sql.c       libpq oracle helpers (Tier B); isolates the libpq
                        dependency.
    prop_<area>.c       one file per area; a set of PG_PROP() properties.
    sql/                query-level areas that generate whole SQL statements
                        (query_gen.c plus the TLP, NoREC, cardinality, PQS, and
                        plan-advice areas).
    t/001_prop.pl       TAP driver for the in-process (Tier A) areas.
    t/002_sql_props.pl  TAP driver for the SQL-oracle (Tier B) areas; starts a
                        node.
    Makefile            autoconf build (one program, pg_prop).
    meson.build         meson build (same program) plus test registration.

All areas compile into one program, pg_prop.  The first command-line argument
selects the area; the rest select individual properties.  One binary (rather
than one per area) keeps the build small and gives the "pg_prop <area>" dispatch
the user-facing CLI wants.


Relationship to TAP: integrated with, not gated by
==================================================

PostgreSQL has three test *harnesses* -- pg_regress (SQL golden-master),
isolation (interleaved sessions), and TAP (Perl scripts emitting the TAP
protocol).  These are harnesses, not separate categories of truth, and the
meson/make runner gives TAP suite grouping, parallelism, per-test timeouts, and
log capture.

Property-based testing is a *methodology*, and the engine is harness-
independent: pg_prop is a standalone C program that emits the TAP protocol and
runs on its own -- ./pg_prop <area> [-n N] [-s SEED] for the in-process (Tier A)
areas, and the same with PGHOST/PGPORT pointed at any cluster for the SQL-oracle
(Tier B) areas.  It needs neither Perl nor the TAP harness to run.

So the suite is *not gated by* TAP.  The build requires only the opt-in flag
(-Dprop_tests=enabled / --with-hegel) and libhegel; it does not require
tap_tests.  When TAP tests are also enabled, the two Perl drivers
(t/001_prop.pl, t/002_sql_props.pl) are additionally registered as the "prop"
meson suite (postgresql:prop) for convenience -- they start a throwaway cluster
and run the binary -- so a reviewer can "meson test --suite prop".  When TAP is
disabled the binary still builds and is run directly; only the convenience
driver registration is skipped.  This is the deliberate design: PBT integrates
with the TAP harness where it helps (test collection, a named suite) without
inheriting TAP as a hard dependency, and without reinventing a fourth test
runner.


The PG_PROP registry
--------------------

Each prop_<area>.c does:

    #define PG_PROP_AREA "path"
    #include "pg_prop.h"

    PG_PROP(canonicalize_idempotent)
    {
        ...
    }

PG_PROP(name) expands to the property function plus a function-pointer
registration run before main() via __attribute__((constructor)).  Each
registration records the area (from PG_PROP_AREA), the name, and the function.
main() then looks up the area named on the command line and runs its properties.
Because registration is a constructor, simply linking an area file into pg_prop
makes its properties available -- there is no central table to edit.


The run loop and failure reporting
----------------------------------

For each selected property, run_property():

1. builds a hegel_settings_t from the CLI (test_cases, seed, verbosity, and a
   database_key of "area/name" so persisted counterexamples are scoped per
   property);

2. starts a run and pulls test cases until the engine signals completion;

3. for each case, run_one_case() sets up a pg_prop_tc and calls the property body
   inside sigsetjmp.  The body reports its outcome by not returning normally:

   * returning normally        -> HEGEL_STATUS_VALID (property held),
   * pg_prop_assume(false)      -> siglongjmp(..., 2) -> HEGEL_STATUS_INVALID
                                   (case discarded),
   * pg_prop_fail(...)          -> siglongjmp(..., 1) -> HEGEL_STATUS_INTERESTING
                                   (counterexample),
   * a draw past the budget     -> siglongjmp -> HEGEL_STATUS_OVERRUN.

   setjmp/longjmp lets pg_prop_fail be called from arbitrarily deep in a property
   body and still be pg_noreturn;

4. after the run, if it failed, replays each counterexample's blob through
   hegel_test_case_from_blob to re-derive the failing message, and prints the
   "falsified:" line plus the "rerun with" blob as TAP diagnostics.

Output is TAP by default (one ok/not ok per property), so the surrounding "meson
test" / "prove" consumes it directly; --no-tap switches to plain text for
interactive use.


Seeds and the example database
------------------------------

If -s is not given, main() picks a seed from the PID and clock and prints
"# seed: N".  A buildfarm failure is therefore always reproducible with -s N.
The example database (where Hegel persists counterexamples so a known failure
replays first) is placed under TESTDATADIR when the TAP wrapper sets it, and
disabled otherwise, so nothing is written into the source tree.


Two tiers
=========

Backend code -- the planner, executor, type I/O -- cannot be linked into a
frontend program, so the harness has two tiers that differ only in how the code
under test is reached.


Tier A: in-process
------------------

src/common and src/port code (path, jsonapi, encoding, unicode_case,
unicode_norm, pglz, base64, prng, crypto, scram, binaryheap, bitutils) is linked
directly into pg_prop and called in the property body.  Each case is a function
call: microseconds.  t/001_prop.pl runs these; no server is needed.

Crash isolation.  Frontend PostgreSQL code reports fatal problems by abort() (a
failed Assert under cassert) or exit().  In-process, either would take down the
whole pg_prop process and lose the counterexample.  pg_prop_run_isolated() forks
a child to run code that might do this and turns a crash, signal, or non-zero
exit into an ordinary value the property can test and pg_prop_fail on -- with
the drawn inputs, generated in the parent, still in hand for shrinking.  (The
current area files mostly do not need it, since their functions return error
codes rather than aborting, but it is there for code that does and for running
under a sanitizer.)


Tier B: SQL oracle
------------------

Backend behavior is tested differentially over libpq against a live node: the
property generates SQL and compares the answer to an oracle that must agree.
pg_prop_sql.c wraps just enough of libpq -- connect, run a scalar/rowset/DDL
query, render the result as a comparable string -- and keeps the libpq
dependency out of the rest of the harness.  t/002_sql_props.pl starts one node,
exports PGHOST/PGPORT, and runs these areas against it.

The oracles are chosen so a disagreement is unambiguously a bug, never a
difference of opinion:

* partprune: a partitioned table with pruning on, the same table with pruning
  off, and an unpartitioned table holding identical rows must all return the
  same result for any query.  Pruning is a performance optimization that must
  never change results.

* scan and join differentials (nbtree, indexscan, indexdiff, joinmethods,
  outerjoin, ...): the same query run under forced scan methods, or forced join
  methods, or a tiny versus ample work_mem, must return identical rows.

* type algebra (interval, numeric, numfmt, float, cash, date, timestamptz, and
  the rest): algebraic identities evaluated in SQL -- round-trips, a = q*b + r,
  distributivity, a total order, text output that re-parses, add/subtract
  inverses -- that the type is required to satisfy for all finite inputs (with
  legitimate overflow skipped, and never silently wrapped).

* structured types (jsonb, jsonpath, array, range, multirange, record,
  tsvector, and friends): round-trips through text and the set/algebra laws of
  the type's operators (containment, union, intersection, key dedup,
  aggregate/unnest inverse, builder/accessor inverse, lexicographic ordering),
  exact because these types normalize on input.

* parsing and formatting (like, tsquery, tochar, bool, char): matching,
  formatting, and parsing cross-checked against independent builtins
  (starts_with, length, an explicit unnest membership scan) and the boolean
  structure of the operators.

Connection hygiene.  A property that fails or is assumed away unwinds by
longjmp, jumping over its own pg_prop_disconnect().  The driver therefore calls
pg_prop_sql_cleanup() after every case, which closes any connection a case left
open; otherwise a long run would exhaust the server's connection slots.  (It is
a no-op for Tier A.)


Query-level areas (sql/)
========================

The highest-value Tier-B areas do not test one function; they test the query
processor.  They live in sql/ and share query_gen.c, which builds a random table
and a random three-valued boolean predicate as SQL text.  Each area wraps that
in a metamorphic oracle -- a relationship that must hold with no independent
reference answer, so a disagreement is unambiguously a server bug:

* query_tlp -- Ternary Logic Partitioning (Rigger and Su, OOPSLA 2020).  For
  any predicate p every row falls in exactly one of WHERE p, WHERE NOT p, WHERE
  p IS NULL, so the three partitions UNION ALL'd must reconstruct the unfiltered
  relation.

* query_tlp_agg -- the same ternary partition applied to aggregates: count(*),
  sum, min and max over the whole table must equal the fold of the three
  partition aggregates.  Exercises the hash/sorted aggregation paths and the
  NULL/empty-group folding that plain row return does not.

* query_tlp_distinct -- the ternary partition under DISTINCT and the set
  operators: DISTINCT distributes over the partition up to UNION, the p and
  NOT-p partitions are disjoint (INTERSECT is empty), and EXCEPT recovers the
  p-IS-NULL partition.  Exercises duplicate elimination and set-operation
  handling.

* query_norec -- compare an optimizable count(*) ... WHERE p against the
  un-optimizable twin that pulls p into a bare per-row scan; the counts must
  match.  Variants cover the FALSE partition, a sum under the filter, and the
  predicate across a self-join (where pushdown interacts with the join).

* query_cardinality -- metamorphic relations on result size rather than
  contents: adding a conjunct cannot grow and adding a disjunct cannot shrink a
  result, the ternary partition tiles the table exactly, DISTINCT never grows a
  projection, and UNION ALL is additive while UNION is no larger.  Cheap (one
  count per query) yet broad.

* query_pqs -- Pivoted Query Synthesis (Rigger and Su, ESEC/FSE 2020).  Pick a
  real pivot row, read its values, synthesize a WHERE predicate guaranteed TRUE
  for it by rectifying every leaf against those values, and require the pivot's
  id in the result.  Unlike the metamorphic oracles it checks a single query
  against a single known-true fact, so it catches a bug that corrupts both
  sides of a comparison identically.

* query_planadvice -- run a generated query with the planner free and again
  steered by contrib/pg_plan_advice (SEQ_SCAN, NESTED_LOOP_PLAIN, JOIN_ORDER,
  and so on, via the pg_plan_advice.advice GUC), plus a tiny-work_mem spill
  variant; the result must not depend on the plan the planner chose.  Advice is
  used in preference to the blunt enable_* GUCs because it names the exact
  relation or join to re-shape and the extension rejects advice it cannot
  honor, so the alternate plan is a targeted legal variant of the same query
  rather than a global cost distortion.

* query_multi -- the oracles above over a multi-table generated database rather
  than a self-joined single table.  qg_build_db() creates 2..N related tables
  with declared primary and foreign keys and correlated data (a child's FK
  column is drawn from the parent's actual key values), so joins have a real
  referential structure for the planner to exploit.  Properties: TLP over a FK
  join, join-cardinality commutativity and the cross-product bound, and that a
  FK equi-join returns exactly the non-NULL-FK child rows.  The generator has
  three profiles selected by the PG_PROP_DEEP environment variable: unset (the
  small, fast, deterministic default the committed TAP run uses); "fast" (a
  leaner deep profile -- a few bounded-size tables, still multi-table with FKs,
  subqueries, TOAST and triggers -- cheap enough to run tens of thousands of
  iterations unattended); and "full" (the maximal profile: many wide tables, up
  to thousands of rows, heavy TOAST, for a short, heavily-resourced run).  The
  deep profiles vary TOAST storage mode per column (PLAIN / MAIN / EXTERNAL /
  EXTENDED) and attach result-neutral BEFORE-row triggers to some tables (they
  return the row unchanged, so SELECT oracles stay sound while the relation
  still carries trigger metadata and the write path runs the trigger).

* query_deep -- the oracles over an arbitrary generated query shape, not just a
  single join.  qg_build_query() walks the foreign-key graph to build a random
  left-deep join tree (inner and left joins) of configurable depth, projecting
  a stable key list and exposing one mutable predicate over the anchor relation
  so a metamorphic oracle can vary just that predicate while the join stays
  fixed.  Properties: deep_tlp (the predicate partitioned three ways
  reconstructs the join), deep_norec (the predicate in WHERE versus the same
  predicate wrapped in a CASE barrier must count the same rows), deep_cte_inline
  (the filtered query as an inline subquery, a plain CTE, and a MATERIALIZED CTE
  must return identical rows -- the subquery-pullup and CTE-inlining rewrites),
  deep_const_fold (ANDing a plan-time-foldable tautology (1=1) and a run-time
  tautology (col - col = 0) must not change the result -- constant folding), and
  deep_corr_subquery (a parent-filtered semijoin written as IN, as EXISTS, and
  as a DISTINCT join must agree -- subquery unnesting).


Trait-based access-method areas
===============================

An access method is described by *traits* -- capabilities it declares and the
server exposes at runtime -- and the tests are organized along them so that the
generic part is reusable by any implementation of the trait, in core or out of
tree.

Index AMs (sql/index_traits.c, driven by sql/prop_index_am.c).  A trait is a
contract that must hold for ANY index AM claiming it: exact (equality equals a
seqscan), ordered (ranges, ORDER BY satisfied by the index, backward scan),
summarizing (BRIN-style lossy block ranges never drop a matching row),
multicol, include (index-only scans), search_nulls, unique.  The harness takes
an am_driver (the AM name, its setup DDL, and the indexed column/opclass) and
runs, for each trait the AM actually claims, that trait's properties -- the
core oracle throughout being differential: force the plan onto the index, force
it onto a seqscan, require identical rows.

Crucially the harness asks the server which traits the AM has, at the right
granularity: pg_indexam_has_property for AM-level traits (can_order, can_unique,
can_multi_col, can_include), pg_index_has_property for index-level ones
(index_scan, bitmap_scan, backward_scan), and pg_index_column_has_property for
column-level ones (asc, orderable, search_nulls).  So prop_index_am.c encodes no
capability matrix -- it supplies one driver per shipped AM (btree, hash, brin,
gin, gist) and the server decides what runs.  That is exactly what lets an
out-of-tree AM reuse the whole applicable suite: BARK, a B+-tree successor to
nbtree, declares amcanorder/amcanunique/amcaninclude in its amhandler and gets
the entire ordered + unique + include + exact + search_nulls trait suite by
adding one am_driver; a summarizing extension AM gets the summarizing suite the
same way.  AM-specific behaviour that is not a shared trait (nbtree skip scan)
stays in its own area (prop_nbtree.c).

Table AMs (sql/prop_table_am.c).  The same split: the generic contract every
table AM must satisfy -- insert/delete/update row-count correctness, MVCC
visibility and rollback, one distinct ctid per live row, aggregates agreeing
with the scan -- is written over "CREATE TABLE ... USING <am>" with the AM named
by PG_PROP_TABLE_AM (default heap).  Another table AM (OrioleDB, the undo-based
FLUX AM) reuses the whole suite by building the binary with its extension loaded
and the AM name supplied; nothing in it is heap-specific.

The older indexdiff area is kept as-is: it tests index *features* orthogonal to
AM traits (partial and expression indexes), which are not AM capabilities, so it
complements rather than duplicates the trait areas.

The bloom contrib AM is driven by the index trait harness too (it declares
bitmap-only, lossy, no ordering), so it exercises the exact and summarizing-
shaped trait checks.  spgist is not in the int-column trait suite (it ships no
integer opclass); it is a candidate for its own area over the scalar types it
supports.


Procedural languages (sql/prop_pl_langs.c)
==========================================

The same pure, deterministic integer computation expressed in each procedural
language -- PL/pgSQL, PL/Python (plpython3u), PL/Perl, PL/Tcl -- must equal the
pure-SQL computation of the same expression.  This is a differential oracle
across language implementations; a disagreement is a bug in a PL's argument
marshalling, integer handling, or return conversion.  Each property probes its
language with pg_prop_require_extension and pg_prop_skips when the server was
not built with it, so the area runs whatever PLs are present and reports the
rest as TAP SKIP rather than failing.


Integrity oracles (sql/prop_integrity.c)
========================================

The integrity-checking contrib extensions make powerful oracles: for any table
and index the generator builds, and after any DML churn it applies, the
structure the server produced must pass the extension's own self-check.  A
failure is a storage- or access-method-level corruption bug -- a different and
valuable class from wrong results.  btree_amcheck (bt_index_check on a freshly
built index), heap_verify (verify_heapam finds no corruption after churn),
vm_consistent (pg_check_visible / pg_check_frozen find no wrongly-set
visibility-map bits after VACUUM FREEZE), and stattuple_bounds (pgstattuple's
byte accounting is non-negative and bounded by the table size).  Each requires
its contrib extension and skips cleanly if absent.  (A check function that
reports via RAISE, like bt_index_check, is invoked as a plain top-level SELECT,
never buried in a subquery a constant-false outer query could optimize away --
that elision would make the oracle vacuous; see finding 024.)


The skip / availability framework
=================================

pg_prop_require_extension(conn, name) checks pg_available_extensions and, if the
module is installable, loads it with CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS, returning
whether it is now present.  pg_prop_skip(tc, fmt, ...) abandons the whole
property and reports it as skipped (TAP "ok N # SKIP reason").  Together they
let an extension or PL area degrade gracefully: it loads what it needs if the
server offers it, and marks itself skipped -- not failed -- when it does not.


Build-system integration
========================

meson
-----

* meson_options.txt adds a feature option prop_tests (default disabled).

* The top-level meson.build, right after the TAP-tests block, resolves
  prop_tests_enabled: it requires tap_tests_enabled, then looks for hegel.h (via
  postgres_inc, which includes --extra-include-dirs) and the hegel_c library
  (via postgres_lib_d, i.e. --extra-lib-dirs).  All three present -> enabled;
  -Dprop_tests=enabled with a piece missing -> hard error; otherwise silently
  off.  A summary() line reports the result.

* src/test/meson.build descends into prop/ only when prop_tests_enabled.
  src/test/prop/meson.build builds pg_prop (linking frontend_code, libpq, and
  libhegel) and registers the two TAP drivers as the "prop" suite.

autoconf
--------

* configure.ac adds --with-hegel (default no); it errors immediately if
  --enable-tap-tests was not also given.  Down with the other library probes
  (where --with-includes/--with-libraries are in effect) it runs
  AC_CHECK_HEADER(hegel.h) and AC_CHECK_LIB(hegel_c, hegel_version), both hard
  errors under --with-hegel.  The AC_CHECK_LIB deliberately does not add
  -lhegel_c to the global LIBS; only pg_prop links it.

* with_hegel flows through Makefile.global.in.  src/test/Makefile adds prop to
  SUBDIRS when with_hegel = yes (and always to ALWAYS_SUBDIRS so "make clean"
  recurses).  src/test/prop/Makefile builds the one program and declares
  TAP_TESTS = 1, so "make check" runs t/*.pl.


Measuring the value: coverage and input diversity
==================================================

Two things were measured on an instrumented build (meson setup ...
-Db_coverage=true), PostgreSQL src/ only, branch coverage off, comparing the PBT
suite against "test-world" (the regress and isolation suites).

Line coverage, the number that undersells it:

    configuration                      line coverage         function coverage
    test-world (regress + isolation)   72.1% (315,641)       77.6%
    PBT suite only                     32.6% (130,227)       42.4%
    union (test-world + PBT)           72.8% (327,606)       79.0%

The PBTs add 1,293 server lines test-world never reaches (+0.41% relative).
That is small, and honestly so: test-world already touches almost every adt/*.c
line at least once.

Execution depth per line, what we hoped for and did not find.  It is tempting to
argue the PBTs at least execute the shared lines far more often -- more inputs
per line.  Measured directly with gcov execution counts, the honest result is
no, not in raw count.  Run for run, the dense regression corpus executes the hot
type-I/O and planner lines more than a 100-iteration PBT pass, and even a single
area at 8,000 iterations executes src/common/jsonapi.c only about 0.2x as many
times total as the json/jsonb regression tests do.  Per hot file the PBT/world
execution-count ratios ran from about 0.1x (formatting.c, selfuncs.c) to about
2.7x (nodeAgg.c); there is no "orders of magnitude" effect and none is claimed.

Input diversity via differential coverage, the real measured gain.  The gain is
on a different axis: the generators reach different lines, not the same lines
more.  Measured on the file each area targets, the jsonapi area at 8,000
iterations covers 527 lines of src/common/jsonapi.c, of which 242 are lines the
json and jsonb regression tests never reach (test-world covers 464).  Those 242
lines are overwhelmingly the incremental-parser state machine, which only
executes when a value is delivered one byte at a time -- precisely what the
generator does and what no hand-written case enumerates.  This is the same
mechanism that caught the reverted jsonapi "0.0" bug.

To reproduce the measurement, build with -Db_coverage=true, zero the counters
(find ... -name '*.gcda' -delete), run one suite, capture with lcov (adding
--ignore-errors inconsistent,corrupt), extract the PostgreSQL src/ paths, repeat
for the other suite, and compare the per-line DA: counts and the covered-line
sets between the two tracefiles.

Function and branch coverage of the query-level areas.  The query-generation
areas (sql/) are measured separately because their value is executing planner
and executor paths, where branch coverage is the metric that matters -- a wrong
turn at a branch is exactly how a plan produces wrong rows.  On a branch-enabled
coverage build (lcov --rc branch_coverage=1), the seven query areas alone,
measured over src/backend/{optimizer,executor,parser} and nbtree, reach:

    subtree                            lines    functions   branches
    optimizer + executor + parser      29.2%    42.3%       20.1%

and drive the specific files the oracles target:

    file                               lines    functions   branches
    optimizer/path/clausesel.c         85.2%    100%        72.4%
    optimizer/prep/prepunion.c         77.3%    91.7%       55.8%
    optimizer/plan/planner.c           43.8%    59.7%       31.0%
    executor/nodeAgg.c                 53.8%    61.1%       36.4%
    executor/nodeNestloop.c            69.2%    75.0%       47.2%
    executor/nodeHashjoin.c            35.0%    35.3%       14.3%
    executor/nodeMergejoin.c           57.6%    63.6%       29.5%

clausesel.c and prepunion.c are high precisely because the predicate generator
and the TLP/distinct set-operation oracles hammer them.  nodeMergejoin.c was at
0% until a plan-advice property steering MERGE_JOIN_PLAIN was added -- branch
coverage, not line or function, is what surfaced that the file was never entered
at all, and the single new property took it to 57.6%/63.6%/29.5%.  The remaining
nodeHashjoin.c branches are the spill paths, which the tiny-work_mem variants
reach only intermittently at this table size.

The takeaway for a reviewer: judge this suite by which code it reaches and by
the standing differential net, not by a coverage-percentage delta or a
depth-per-line multiplier -- the latter does not favor PBT and we do not claim
it does.

Whole-suite lift over the regression tests.  On a branch-coverage build,
measured over src/backend + src/common, the core regression suite alone covers
70.0% of lines, 73.3% of functions and 53.4% of branches; adding the full PBT
suite on top raises that to 72.0% / 76.3% / 54.7%.  The property suite executes
544 backend/common functions and 7,933 lines the entire regression suite never
reaches, plus 3,082 additional branches.  Branch coverage moves least, as
expected -- the regression suite already drives most hot branches, so the PBT
win is breadth (new functions and lines, in the incremental jsonapi parser, the
access-method trait paths, and the deep planner transformations) more than
depth on branches already covered.  This is the concrete argument for carrying
the suite in core: not to replace the golden-master tests (the union is what
matters) but to reach a standing slice of the backend they structurally do not,
with generated rather than hand-enumerated inputs.


Why this shape
==============

* One binary, area-dispatched.  Matches the "pg_prop <area>" CLI, keeps the
  build to a single target, and lets a developer run or debug exactly one
  property with a fixed seed.

* C ABI, not the C++ frontend.  Keeps the tree C-only; the only non-C artifact
  is the prebuilt engine library itself.

* setjmp for fail/assume.  Lets assertions sit naturally deep in a property body
  and keeps pg_prop_fail pg_noreturn.

* Differential oracles for backend code.  Avoids running the engine inside a
  backend (which would need PG_TRY shims to keep longjmp out of the engine's
  frames -- not needed here, and not worth the complexity until a round-trip
  through SQL becomes a measured bottleneck).

* Default 100 iterations.  Keeps check-world cheap; the iteration count is a knob
  (-n / PG_PROP_ITERATIONS) you turn up when hunting.


Limitations and future work
===========================

* Platform coverage is whatever libhegel ships a prebuilt library for (Linux
  x86_64/arm64, macOS arm64, Windows at the time of writing); other platforms
  would need a source build of the engine.  The opt-in default keeps this from
  affecting anyone who does not ask for it.

* Beta engine.  Hegel is pre-1.0; its API can change and replay blobs are only
  guaranteed to reproduce on the same engine version.  The configure checks pin
  to the header and library actually installed.

* In-backend properties (running the engine inside a running backend, for
  cheaper backend coverage than round-tripping SQL) are deliberately not
  attempted yet; they would require PG_TRY-based shims so a longjmp cannot cross
  the engine's frames.  Worth revisiting only if the SQL round-trip cost becomes
  the bottleneck.

* Stateful and model testing (sequences of operations checked against a
  reference model) is supported by Hegel but not yet used here; the data
  structures in src/backend/lib (for example the radix tree and the TID store)
  are the natural first customers.

* More query-generation oracles remain a growth area.  Implemented so far: TLP
  (rows, aggregates, DISTINCT/set-ops), NoREC, plan-advice metamorphic testing
  (scan/join method, join order, spill), result-cardinality monotonicity, and
  Pivoted Query Synthesis.  Still open and likely to find a live wrong-results
  bug: a multi-table generator for richer join-order and subquery-unnesting
  equivalence, constant-folding metamorphic checks, and correlated-subquery
  rewrites.  A larger/deeper generator (wider joins, more columns, correlated
  data) run at high iteration is the lever most likely to turn one up.
